---
### Regular Expressions
...
{size=32} A {{color=emp|regular expression}} is a notation for a well-defined set of "words" (called a "regular language")
...
{size=32} If {{color=id|$A$}} is a regular expression, then {{color=id|$L(A)$}} is the language defined by that regular expression.
...
{size=32} {{color=id|$L(``c")$}} is a language with the single word {{color=id|"c"}}.
...
{size=32} But how do we specify the details of these regular expressions?
---
### Regular Expressions: **Concatenation**
...
{size=32} {{color=emp|$L(AB) = \{ ab : a ∈ L(A) \text{ and } b ∈ L(B) \}$}}
...
{size=32} If two regular expressions are concatenated, then both must be satisfied, one then the other.
...
{size=32} {{color=id|$L$('i' 'f')}} is a language with just the single word “{{color=keyword|if}}” in it
...
{size=32} {{color=id|$L$(‘ w ' ‘h’ ‘ i ’ ‘l’ ‘e')}} is a language with just the single word “{{color=keyword|while}}”
...
{size=32} These regex’s would usually be written as just {{color=num|`if`}} and {{color=num|`while`}}.
---
### Regular Expressions: **Union**
...
{size=32} {{color=emp|$L(A | B) = \{ s : s ∈ L(A) \text{ or } s ∈ L(B) \}$}}
...
{size=32} If two regular expressions are joined with a union (the pipe ‘|’ symbol, “OR”) , then either can be satisfied.
...
{size=32} {{color=id|$L( \text{ if } | \text{ then } | \text{ else })$}} is a language with the three words “{{color=keyword|if}}”, “{{color=keyword|then}}” and “{{color=keyword|else}}” in it.
...
{size=32} {{color=id|$L( (a | b) (c | d) )$}} is a language consisting of the four words: “ {{color=num|ac}} ”, “ {{color=num|ad}} ”, “ {{color=num|bc}} ”, and “ {{color=num|bd}} ”
---
### Regular Expressions: **Other patterns**
| Expression | Meaning |
|:---:|:---|
| **"A"** | **A** is taken **literally** (e.g., "*" is the star symbol, not repeat). |
| **A?** | **A** can be repeated **zero or one times**. |
| **A+** | **A** can be repeated **one or more times**. |
| **A*** | **A** can be repeated **zero or more times**. |
| **(A)** | **A** should be grouped as a **single expression** |
| **[…]** | Match any single character between **[** and **]**.  Start with `^` to negate; use `-` for range. |
| **A{** ***n*** **}** | **A** must be matched **exactly** ***n*** **times**. |
| **A{** ***n,*** **}** | **A** must be matched **at least** ***n*** **times**. |
| **A{** ***m,n*** **}** | **A** must be matched **at least** ***m*** **times and at most** *n* **times**. |

