---
### Regular Expressions: **Character Classes**
{size=30} Square brackets define a {{color=emp|character class}}: a set from which exactly {{color=num|one}} character is matched.

| Expression | Matches |
|:---:|:---|
| **[abc]** | One character: `a`, `b`, or `c` |
| **[a-z]** | A **range** — any one lower-case letter |
| **[a-zA-Z_]** | Ranges and single characters combine freely |
| **[^abc]** | Negated — any one character that is **not** `a`, `b`, or `c` |
| **[-+]** | A `-` is literal when it is **first** or **last** in the class |

...
{size=30} Emplex provides shortcuts for the most common classes: {{color=regex|`\d`}} = `[0-9]`, {{color=regex|`\l`}} = `[a-zA-Z]`, {{color=regex|`\w`}} = `[A-Za-z0-9_]`, {{color=regex|`\s`}} = whitespace, and {{color=regex|`.`}} = `[^\n]`.  Capitalize to negate: {{color=regex|`\D`}}, {{color=regex|`\L`}}, {{color=regex|`\W`}}, {{color=regex|`\S`}}.
...
{size=30} A class always matches {{color=num|one}} character, so use `*` or `+` when you want a run of them: {{color=regex|`[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*`}}
...
{size=30} Negated classes are how you say {{color=emp|"everything up to a delimiter"}}.  For example, {{color=regex|`//[^\n]*`}} matches a line comment: any run of characters that stops at the end of the line.
...
{size=30} {{color=math|Your turn}}: how would you match a literal string in quotes?  Which characters must the class exclude, and what breaks if you forget one?
