### Designing a regular expression for literal numbers
!set tabx 220
!set col1 50
!set col2 670
!set spacing 45
!set re_size 28
!set txt_size 28

@(${col1},${tabx}) {size=32} **Regular Expression**
@(${col2},${tabx}) {size=32} **Issue (and example of failed case)**
...
@(${col1},${tabx+spacing}) {size=${re_size},color=regex,font=monospace} [0-9]
...
@(${col2},${tabx+spacing}) {size=${txt_size}} Only single digit (not “ **314** ”)
...
@(${col1},${tabx+spacing*2}) {size=${re_size},color=regex,font=monospace} [0-9]{{color=math|+}}
...
@(${col2},${tabx+spacing*2}) {size=${txt_size}} Non-negative numbers (not **-314** )
...
@(${col1},${tabx+spacing*3}) {size=${re_size},color=regex,font=monospace} {{color=math|[-+]?}}[0-9]+
...
@(${col2},${tabx+spacing*3}) {size=${txt_size}} No floating point (not **3.14** )
...
@(${col1},${tabx+spacing*4}) {size=${re_size},color=regex,font=monospace} [-+]?[0-9]+{{color=math|\.[0-9]+}}
...
@(${col2},${tabx+spacing*4}) {size=${txt_size}} **Requires** decimal point (not **314** )
...
@(${col1},${tabx+spacing*5}) {size=${re_size},color=regex,font=monospace} [-+]?[0-9]+{{color=math|(}}\.[0-9]+{{color=math|)?}}
...
@(${col2},${tabx+spacing*5}) {size=${txt_size}} Needs digit **before** decimal point (not **.314** )
...
@(${col1},${tabx+spacing*6}) {size=${re_size},color=regex,font=monospace} [-+]?{{color=math|(}}[0-9]+(\.[0-9]+)?{{color=math|)|(\.[0-9]+)}}
...
@(${col2},${tabx+spacing*6}) {size=${txt_size}} Needs digits **after** decimal point (not **314.** )
...
@(${col1},${tabx+spacing*7}) {size=${re_size},color=regex,font=monospace} [-+]?([0-9]+(\.[0-9]{{color=math|*}})?)|(\.[0-9]+)
...
@(${col2},${tabx+spacing*7}) {size=${txt_size}} Can be simplified a bit…
...
@(${col1},${tabx+spacing*8}) {size=${re_size},color=regex,font=monospace} [-+]?([0-9]{{color=#FF84FF|+\}}.{{color=math|?}}[0-9]*{{color=#FF84FF|)|}}(\.[0-9]+)
...
@(${col2},${tabx+spacing*8}) {size=${txt_size}} No scientific notation… (not **9.8e-7** )
...
@(60,645) {size=36, w=1200,color=emp,font=monospace} [-+]?(([0-9]+\.?[0-9]*)|(\.[0-9]+))([eE][-+]?[0-9]+)?

---
### Identifying Tokens
{size=32} A lexical analyzer must be able to do three things:
- {size=32} Correctly identify all tokens within a string.
- {size=32} Discard all tokens for whitespace and comments.
- {size=32} Return each remaining token with its type , its lexeme and the line number it was found on.
{size=32} How do we go about implementing this?

---
### Can we just search for one token type at a time?
...
&nbsp;
{size=36px}Consider searching for a literal number:
...
[l1] {size=36,w=1200,color=emp,font=monospace} &nbsp;int my_var2;
[l2] {size=36,w=1200,color=emp,font=monospace} &nbsp;string test_str = “This is test #12”;
[l3] {size=36,w=1200,color=emp,font=monospace} &nbsp;x = 23; // Set x to the value 23.
...
!anim l1 text &nbsp;int my_var{{color=warn|2}};
...
!anim l2 text &nbsp;string test_str = “This is test #{{color=warn|12}}”;
...
!anim l3 text &nbsp;x = {{color=#0096FF|23}}; // Set x to the value 23.
...
!anim l3 text &nbsp;x = {{color=#0096FF|23}}; // Set x to the value {{color=warn|23}}.
...
@(80, 500) {w=1196px, size=36px} Instead, we must step through input characters sequentially, matching regular expressions as we go.
---
### Lookahead
...
{size=36} - When reading tokens left-to-right, it is not always possible to decide if a token is finished without looking ahead.
...
{size=36} - Is '{{color=id|i}}' a variable, or the first character of the keyword '{{color=id|if}}' or '{{color=id|int}}', or is it some other identifier?
...
{size=36} - Is '{{color=math|=}}' an assignment, or the beginning of a comparison '{{color=math|==}}'?
---
### Lookahead
...
{size=36} - Some languages require more lookahead than others. For example, Fortran removes all whitespace before processing.
...
    [c1] {size=36,font=monospace} - DO 5 I = 1,25
...
{size=36} - ' DO ' is a keyword ! It triggers the variable I to go from 1 to 25, with each iteration looping back when it hits the line labeled “5”.
...
!anim c1 text {{color=keyword|DO}} {{color=num|5}} {{color=id|I}} {{color=math|=}} {{color=num|1}}{{color=math|,}}{{color=num|25}}
...
    [c2] {size=36,font=monospace} - DO 5 I = 1.25
...
{size=36} - ' DO5I ' is a variable ! It is initialized to the value 1.25
...
!anim c2 text {{color=id|DO 5 I}} {{color=math|=}} {{color=num|1.25}}

---
### Uglier Lookahead example
PL/I is another difficult-to-lex language because it allows identifiers to be the same as keywords.
- Consider these *legal* statements:
[l1] &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;IF THEN THEN THEN = ELSE;
[l2] &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;ELSE ELSE = THEN;
...
- ELSE and THEN are {{color=keyword|**keywords**}} in some contexts, but are {{color=id|**variables**}} in other contexts.
...
!anim l1 text &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;{{color=keyword|IF}} {{color=id|THEN}} {{color=keyword|THEN}} {{color=id|THEN}} {{color=math|=}} {{color=id|ELSE}};
!anim l2 text &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;{{color=keyword|ELSE}} {{color=id|ELSE}} {{color=math|=}} {{color=id|THEN}};

---
### Details...
...
{size=36} - How should we figure out ambiguities?
...
{size=36} - If we see 'intact', are we declaring an integer called 'act' or making use of a previously defined identifier by the name 'intact'?
...
{size=36} - We need rules that ensure only one possible answer, ideally with only one character of lookahead.
---
### Designing Regular Expressions for a Lexer
...
{size=36} A regular expression must {{color=#00F900|cover ALL cases}} for the token type. If there are multiple distinct ways of building a token, use "or" ("|").
...
{size=36} A regular expression must {{color=#FF0000|exclude any cases}} that do not fit the given token type *unless* another RE will have priority
...
{size=36} The first method of deciding between two regular expressions is to take the {{color=id|longer match}}. (“{{color=num|maximum munch}}”)
...
{size=36} Equal-length matches are decided by taking the {{color=id|first match}} in order.
...
{size=36} If {{color=warn|no}} RegEx matches, the lexer returns that {{color=id|single character}} as its own token, and lets the next stage report the problem.
---
### Building a Lexer
{size=36} Consider a language with the following token types:
[id1] @(150,220) {size=36} {{color=num|Identifier}}:
[id2] @(150,280) {size=36} {{color=num|Number}}:
[id3] @(150,340) {size=36} {{color=num|Keywords}}:
[id4] @(150,400) {size=36} {{color=num|Comparison}}:

[re1] @(400,220) {size=36,font=monospace,color=pink} {{color=#9090FF|[a-zA-Z0-9_]}}+
[re2] @(400,280) {size=36,font=monospace,color=pink} {{color=#9090FF|[-+]}}?{{color=#9090FF|[0-9]}}+({{color=emp|\.}}{{color=#9090FF|[0-9]}}+)?
[re3] @(400,340) {size=36,font=monospace,color=pink} {{color=emp|if}}|{{color=emp|else}}|{{color=emp|int}}|{{color=emp|float}}|{{color=emp|for}}|{{color=emp|while}}
[re4] @(400,400) {size=36,font=monospace,color=pink} ({{color=#9090FF|[=!]}}{{color=emp|=}})|({{color=emp|<=}}?)|({{color=emp|>=}}?)
...
@(80, 480) {color=#b8dcab, size=36px} What are the issues with these lexer patterns?
...
[question] @(80, 560) {color=#b8dcab, size=36px}  What would “**`100`**” match?
...
